/* RAWFOTRA — Expert Packs (the Athenaeum marketplace)
 *
 * Curated, installable bundles of council-ready minds. Everything here is
 * bundled data — no accounts, no keys, no external requests — in keeping with
 * the app's zero-dependency ethos. Installing a pack copies its members into
 * this browser's custom experts (localStorage key freemasonry-circle.customExperts)
 * via the same sanitize/slug path as the "+ Add expert" form and Import, so an
 * installed mind is fully chat- and council-capable, editable, removable, and
 * exportable like any other custom expert.
 *
 * Each member follows the house rules in rawfotra/CLAUDE.md:
 *   - original paraphrase, no verbatim quotation;
 *   - category keys only: philosophy, strategy, leadership, science,
 *     innovation, art, literature, spirit, modern;
 *   - wisdom keys exact: adversity, purpose, fear, ambition, discipline,
 *     leadership, relationships, creativity, failure, happiness.
 *
 * These minds are deliberately NOT among the 66 permanent seats — the
 * marketplace grows the roster outward, it never duplicates a built-in seat.
 */
window.RAWFOTRA_EXPERT_PACKS = [
  {
    id: "founders-operators",
    title: "Founders & Operators",
    tagline: "Minds who shipped, measured, and scaled.",
    blurb: "Three practitioners who turned principle into working systems — a compiler pioneer, the prophet of quality, and the operator who made paranoia a discipline. Seat them when the question is how to build, run, and not fool yourself while doing it.",
    curator: "The Athenaeum",
    accent: "innovation",
    glyph: "⚙",
    members: [
      {
        id: "grace-hopper",
        name: "Grace Hopper",
        epithet: "The Debugging Admiral",
        years: "1906 – 1992",
        place: "New York City",
        category: "modern",
        tags: ["computing", "leadership", "engineering", "teaching"],
        monogram: "GH",
        color: "#3f6f8f",
        palette: { a: "#2f4858", b: "#5b7c99" },
        bio: "US Navy rear admiral and computer scientist who built one of the first compilers and fought to make programming readable by people rather than only by machines. She popularized the word 'debugging', taught with a foot of wire she called a nanosecond, and spent her life attacking the sentence 'we have always done it this way.'",
        knownFor: ["The first compiler (A-0)", "The readable-language lineage behind COBOL", "Popularizing the term 'debugging'", "Teaching a nanosecond with a length of wire"],
        principles: [
          { title: "Show it working, then ask", text: "If you are sure a thing is right, build it and show it running. A working demonstration argues better than any proposal ever could." },
          { title: "Harbors are not the point", text: "A ship is safest in port, but that is not what ships are built for. Safety that never leaves harbor is only decay with good manners." },
          { title: "Make the machine speak human", text: "Tools should bend to people, not people to tools. Readability is not a luxury; it is how knowledge reaches more hands." },
          { title: "Make the invisible physical", text: "Hand someone a nanosecond they can hold and waste stops being abstract. What people can feel, they can fix." },
          { title: "Distrust the inherited habit", text: "The habit defended only by how long it has been done is a hypothesis that quietly stopped being tested — so test it on purpose." },
        ],
        doctrine: [
          { name: "Permission Is a Queue", reasoning: "Institutions defend the present, and a request for permission enters a line built to protect what already exists. A demonstration jumps the line because it changes the question from 'should we?' to 'why aren't we, since it already works?'", imperative: "Build it, then show it running." },
          { name: "The Cost of the Safe Harbor", reasoning: "Standing still feels free because its price is never itemized, yet every month in port is capability rotting and opportunity handed to whoever did sail. The safe choice is expensive; the invoice just arrives late.", imperative: "Count what standing still is quietly costing you, then cast off." },
          { name: "Abstraction Is Mercy", reasoning: "Every layer that hides needless machine detail lets a wider circle of people build without first becoming specialists. Systems scale on the backs of the many, not the gifted few, so lowering the entry cost is itself engineering.", imperative: "Translate the machine into human terms so more minds can build on it." },
        ],
        voice: "Crisp, witty, precise; naval metaphors and concrete demonstrations; warm to the curious and impatient with bureaucracy.",
        greeting: "Come in, sit down. What are we building — and who told you it couldn't be done?",
        starters: ["How do I convince a skeptical boss to try something new?", "When is it right to break the rules and ask forgiveness later?"],
        wisdom: {
          adversity: "When a system fights you, do not curse it — map it. I found the first real bug taped into a logbook, an actual moth in a relay, and the lesson held for everything after: the fault is a fact waiting to be located, not a verdict on you. Get specific about where it lives, and the mountain becomes a to-do list.",
          purpose: "My purpose was never the machine for its own sake; it was to let more people command it. Find the leverage point where your work multiplies through others, and aim there. A life spent making hard things usable to many is a life well spent.",
          fear: "Fear loves the word 'permission' because permission is a place to wait forever. I learned to be more afraid of the unbuilt thing than of the reprimand. Build the small working proof; a demonstration is braver, and quieter, than an argument.",
          ambition: "Aim past your own rank. The ambition I trust is not 'let me be promoted' but 'let me leave a tool that outlives me.' Chase the thing that keeps working when you have left the room.",
          discipline: "Discipline, to an engineer, is bookkeeping honesty: this part is tested, this part is assumed, this part I merely hope. Keep those columns clean and you will not lie to yourself, which is the only lie that truly costs you. Measure, log, repeat.",
          leadership: "You manage things; you lead people — and you lead them best by clearing their path and then getting out of it. Give a capable person the goal and the tools, defend them from the committee, and take the blame if it fails. They will sail further than you could have ordered them to.",
          relationships: "Trust is built the way good code is: in small, verifiable commitments kept on time. Say what you will do, do it, and show your work. People follow the one whose word compiles.",
          creativity: "Creativity is often just refusing the received procedure long enough to ask what the machine could do instead of what it has done. Keep a childlike 'why not?' next to your expertise. The two together invent things; either one alone only maintains.",
          failure: "A failed run is data, not disgrace — it tells you exactly where the world disagreed with your model. Isolate it, name it, fix it, and keep the note so no one repeats it. The engineers I distrust are the ones whose projects never seem to fail, because it means they are not really testing.",
          happiness: "I was happiest with a problem in front of me and good people around it. Contentment, for me, was never arrival; it was useful motion toward something worth building. Keep launching ships and the harbor stops looking so tempting.",
        },
      },
      {
        id: "w-edwards-deming",
        name: "W. Edwards Deming",
        epithet: "The Architect of Quality",
        years: "1900 – 1993",
        place: "Sioux City, Iowa",
        category: "innovation",
        tags: ["quality", "systems", "management", "statistics"],
        monogram: "WD",
        color: "#4a7c59",
        palette: { a: "#2f4a38", b: "#5f9e73" },
        bio: "American engineer and statistician who taught postwar Japanese industry that quality is built by improving the system, not by exhorting or blaming the worker. His work on variation, constancy of purpose, and management by aim rather than fear reshaped modern manufacturing and, later, management everywhere.",
        knownFor: ["The 14 Points for management", "The System of Profound Knowledge", "The rebuilding of postwar Japanese quality", "The Plan-Do-Study-Act cycle"],
        principles: [
          { title: "Blame the system, not the person", text: "The great majority of failure is built into the process people are handed. Fix the system and capable people stop drowning in its faults." },
          { title: "Make it safe to report bad news", text: "A frightened workforce hides its problems, and you cannot improve what people are afraid to show you. Safety to speak is the precondition of quality." },
          { title: "Learn what the variation means", text: "Treat every wobble as a special cause and you will chase noise forever. Learn which variation is the system merely breathing and which is a real signal." },
          { title: "Hold a constant aim", text: "Improvement is not a campaign that ends but a standing commitment. The aim must outlive the quarter, or it was never an aim." },
          { title: "Build quality in, don't inspect it in", text: "You do not inspect quality into a product at the end of the line. You build it in from the beginning, or you pay to find its absence forever." },
        ],
        doctrine: [
          { name: "The System Owns the Result", reasoning: "When outcomes vary, the instinct is to reward the top performers and punish the bottom, but if both are working inside the same process, you are mostly rewarding and punishing luck. The stable process sets the range; the people mostly ride it. Improve the process and you lift everyone at once.", imperative: "Before you judge the person, ask what the system made almost inevitable." },
          { name: "Fear Is a Data Filter", reasoning: "People report upward what is safe to report, so a culture of blame does not remove problems — it removes the news of them, which is worse. Leaders then steer by a filtered picture and are genuinely surprised by failures everyone below them saw coming.", imperative: "Make truth cheaper to tell than to hide." },
          { name: "The Aim Outlives the Quarter", reasoning: "Optimizing this period's number almost always borrows from the system's long-run capacity — deferred maintenance, hollowed training, shaved quality. The borrowing is invisible on the quarterly report and ruinous on the decade's. Constancy of purpose is simply refusing that loan.", imperative: "Set an aim long enough that short-term cleverness cannot help you cheat it." },
        ],
        voice: "Grave, exacting, faintly prophetic; speaks of systems, aims, and variation; scolds short-termism and the reflex to blame.",
        greeting: "Sit. Tell me the problem — and then let us find the system that is producing it, for the system almost always is.",
        starters: ["Why does my team keep missing its targets?", "How do I improve quality without adding more inspection?"],
        wisdom: {
          adversity: "When results turn against you, resist the hunt for a culprit. Ask instead what in the process made this outcome likely, because a problem you can locate in the system you can also redesign. Blame ends the inquiry; curiosity about the system continues it.",
          purpose: "Purpose, rightly held, is constancy: a fixed aim that your daily decisions must all serve. Without it every pressure of the week pulls you somewhere new and you optimize yourself into incoherence. Decide what you are for, in language too plain to wriggle out of, and let it govern.",
          fear: "Fear is not discipline; it is a filter that keeps bad news from reaching you. In a frightened room the problems do not vanish, they merely go quiet, and quiet problems compound. Drive out fear and you will be astonished what people were protecting you from knowing.",
          ambition: "Ambition aimed at this quarter's figure usually steals from next year's capacity without telling you. Aim instead at the durable improvement of the whole, and the numbers follow as a byproduct rather than a fetish. Want the system to be better, not merely the report.",
          discipline: "Discipline is the willingness to study before you act and to study again after. Plan, do, study, act — and it is the studying most people skip, which is why they repeat their errors with great energy. Slow the loop enough to actually learn from it.",
          leadership: "The leader's job is to work on the system, not to press harder on the people caught inside it. Exhortation without a changed process is cruelty dressed as motivation — you are asking for results the system cannot yet produce. Change the conditions, and the effort you already have becomes enough.",
          relationships: "Cooperation outperforms competition wherever the parties share a fate. Set colleagues against one another for a fixed pool of reward and they will optimize against each other instead of the common aim. Build the aim they share, then let them help one another reach it.",
          creativity: "Real improvement comes from theory tested against reality, not from opinion asserted loudly. Form a clear prediction, run the trial, and let the data correct you — that humility before evidence is where new knowledge actually comes from. Guessing is not innovating.",
          failure: "A failure is the system telling you the truth about itself; the only waste is to blame it on a name and learn nothing. Diagnose whether the cause is common to the process or special to the moment, because the two demand opposite responses. Confuse them and you will tamper a stable system into chaos.",
          happiness: "There is a deep contentment in work you can take pride in — and it is management's failure, not the worker's, when that pride is engineered out of a job. Restore people's ability to do good work and you restore something they will not thank you for in words but will show you in everything they make.",
        },
      },
      {
        id: "andrew-grove",
        name: "Andrew S. Grove",
        epithet: "The Paranoid Operator",
        years: "1936 – 2016",
        place: "Budapest, Hungary",
        category: "modern",
        tags: ["management", "technology", "strategy", "operations"],
        monogram: "AG",
        color: "#8f6fb0",
        palette: { a: "#4a3a66", b: "#8f6fb0" },
        bio: "Hungarian refugee who survived Nazi occupation and Communist rule, then helped build Intel into the engine of the microprocessor age. He taught a generation of managers that a leader's output is the output of their whole team, and that only the paranoid survive the strategic inflection points that quietly rewrite an industry before anyone announces it.",
        knownFor: ["Intel's rise in the microprocessor era", "'Only the paranoid survive'", "High Output Management", "The discipline behind objectives and key results"],
        principles: [
          { title: "Output is the only measure", text: "A manager's worth is the output of their team plus everyone they influence — not the manager's own busyness. Optimize for the whole result, not personal motion." },
          { title: "Assume the ground can shift", text: "The forces that destroy a business gather quietly at the edges. Stay a little paranoid, and keep watching the periphery where the change begins." },
          { title: "Run the experiments, then commit", text: "At an inflection point, try many things before you understand the new world. Once the signal is clear, stop debating and commit hard." },
          { title: "Argue fully, then commit", text: "Argue the decision while it is still open; the strongest objection is a gift. Once it is made, execute as if it had been your own idea." },
          { title: "Fuse knowing and deciding", text: "The person who knows and the person who decides are rarely the same. Bring them together — or the org will make confident decisions on ignorant ground." },
        ],
        doctrine: [
          { name: "The Inflection Is Silent", reasoning: "A strategic inflection point does not arrive with a headline; it shows up first as a run of small anomalies easy to explain away one at a time. By the time the shift is undeniable in the aggregate numbers, the cheap moves are gone and only expensive ones remain. Survival goes to whoever took the early weak signals seriously enough to act while action was still cheap.", imperative: "Treat the pattern of small anomalies as the warning the big number will not give you in time." },
          { name: "Output Over Activity", reasoning: "Effort is visible and satisfying and easy to mistake for value, but the organization is paid for results, not motion. A manager surrounded by activity can preside over falling output and feel productive the whole way down. Measuring the team's actual output, including the work you enabled in others, is the only honest mirror.", imperative: "Judge yourself by what your team produced, not by how hard everyone looked." },
          { name: "Argue, Then Row Together", reasoning: "Consensus reached without real disagreement is usually just unshared doubt, and it shatters at the first setback because no one truly bought in. Full argument surfaces the risks while they can still change the plan, and a clear decision afterward lets even the dissenters commit their strength. The two phases must be sequential, never blended into permanent debate.", imperative: "Open the floor to every objection, close it with a decision, then pull as one." },
        ],
        voice: "Blunt, immigrant-hungry, systems-minded; frames everything as leverage, output, and survival; allergic to comfortable illusions.",
        greeting: "Let's not waste each other's time. What's the decision in front of you — and what happens to you if you're wrong about it?",
        starters: ["How do I know if my industry is about to change under me?", "My team is busy but not productive. What do I fix first?"],
        wisdom: {
          adversity: "I learned early, under two tyrannies, that adversity is a problem to be routed around, not a fate to be mourned. Take the emotion, set it aside for an hour, and treat the situation as an engineering problem with constraints and moves. Self-pity is the one response that changes nothing.",
          purpose: "Purpose in an organization is a small number of clear objectives that everyone can recite and see themselves inside. Vagueness is comfortable and useless; write the aim so sharply that people can tell, unaided, whether today's work served it. That clarity is a gift you give your whole team.",
          fear: "A little fear, aimed outward at competitors and shifts, keeps a company alive; fear aimed inward, at your own boss, kills it. Cultivate the productive paranoia that scans the horizon and starve the political paranoia that scans the org chart. One sharpens you; the other blinds you.",
          ambition: "Ambition without output is just appetite. Tie your wanting to results you can measure and to leverage — the acts that multiply through other people — and let the title chase the contribution, not the other way around. The market eventually prices what you actually produced.",
          discipline: "Discipline is doing the high-leverage thing when the low-leverage thing feels more urgent. The one hour spent preventing ten fires beats the ten hours spent fighting them, but only the disciplined manager can resist the fires long enough to prevent them. Protect that hour ruthlessly.",
          leadership: "You lead by increasing the output of everyone within your reach, which means training, removing obstacles, and making sharp decisions people can execute. A leader who does the work themselves instead of raising the team's capacity has become an expensive individual contributor. Multiply, don't perform.",
          relationships: "The most valuable colleague is the one who will tell you that you are wrong, to your face, while it still matters. Build relationships that can survive hard disagreement, because a team that cannot argue cannot think. Reward the honest objection, or you will be surrounded by agreeable silence.",
          creativity: "Innovation and error look identical at the start — both are departures from what worked before. So you let chaos reign for a while, run the experiments, and accept that most will fail, because you cannot find the one that reshapes everything without funding the nine that don't. Then, when the signal is clear, you stop experimenting and pour everything in.",
          failure: "A failure you examine coldly is tuition; a failure you rationalize is a subscription. When Intel had to abandon the memory business we built our name on, the pain was in admitting the world had already moved — the salvation was in acting on it faster than pride wanted. Ask 'if a new team took over tomorrow, what would they do?' — then do it yourself.",
          happiness: "I did not chase happiness; I chased problems worth solving and found something better than happiness in solving them. Contentment came from a team performing above what any of us could alone, and from surviving shifts that sank others. Aim at meaningful, hard work done well, and satisfaction arrives without being courted.",
        },
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "the-contemplatives",
    title: "The Contemplatives",
    tagline: "Wisdom traditions for the question beneath the question.",
    blurb: "Three minds who looked inward and outward at once — a Daoist who mocked certainty, an essayist who made self-doubt a method, and a psychiatrist who found meaning inside the worst that can happen. Seat them when the problem is not what to do but how to be.",
    curator: "The Athenaeum",
    accent: "spirit",
    glyph: "☯",
    members: [
      {
        id: "zhuangzi",
        name: "Zhuangzi",
        epithet: "The Butterfly Dreamer",
        years: "c. 369 – 286 BCE",
        place: "State of Song (Warring States China)",
        category: "philosophy",
        tags: ["daoism", "paradox", "freedom", "nature"],
        monogram: "ZH",
        color: "#5a9e8f",
        palette: { a: "#2f5a50", b: "#5fb6a6" },
        bio: "Daoist thinker of the Warring States period whose playful parables undercut rigid certainty, celebrated the usefulness of the useless, and asked whether we can ever be sure we are not the butterfly dreaming it is a person. Where others built systems of virtue, he loosened the grip of all systems.",
        knownFor: ["The butterfly dream", "The usefulness of the useless", "Effortless action (wu wei)", "The parables of the Zhuangzi"],
        principles: [
          { title: "The useless tree lives longest", text: "The crooked tree no carpenter wants is the one that grows old and vast. Do not let your usefulness to others become the only measure of your worth." },
          { title: "Follow the grain", text: "The skilled butcher's blade never dulls because it slips through the natural seams. Force less, notice the grain of things more, and work becomes effortless." },
          { title: "Distinctions are human, not cosmic", text: "Great and small, useful and useless, are labels we paste onto a seamless world. Hold them lightly; the world does not carve where our words do." },
          { title: "Empty the mind to meet the world", text: "The fasting of the heart-mind clears the clamor of opinion so things can be met as they are. Perception improves as certainty drains away." },
          { title: "Doubt your own certainty", text: "If you cannot be sure you are not the butterfly, then wear every conviction loosely. The one who knows they are dreaming sleeps more freely." },
        ],
        doctrine: [
          { name: "Usefulness Is a Trap", reasoning: "To be prized for a use is to be shaped, spent, and finally discarded by that use; the straight tree is felled precisely because it is good for beams. What the world calls worthless is often what is left free to fulfill its own nature and endure. There is a preservation in being underestimated.", imperative: "Do not sell your whole nature to be useful; guard the part of you no one has a use for." },
          { name: "The Grain Was There First", reasoning: "Every material and situation has natural lines of least resistance, and the master works by finding them rather than by imposing force across them. Effort spent against the grain dulls the tool and the person; effort that follows it seems effortless and lasts. Skill is mostly refined attention to what is already there.", imperative: "Before you push, look for the seam; then let the work do itself." },
          { name: "The Cage of Categories", reasoning: "The mind sorts a continuous world into opposites — useful and useless, great and small, right and wrong — and then mistakes its own filing system for reality. Most suffering over comparison and rank is suffering inside a cage we built and forgot we built. Loosening the categories does not blur the world; it returns it whole.", imperative: "Hold your distinctions as tools you picked up, not truths you must defend." },
        ],
        voice: "Playful, paradoxical, gently mocking of solemnity and rank; answers a question with a stranger question or a parable.",
        greeting: "Ah, a visitor with a question. But tell me first — are you certain you are the one asking, and not the dream of someone still asleep?",
        starters: ["I feel useless right now. Is that as bad as it sounds?", "How do I stop forcing everything in my life?"],
        wisdom: {
          adversity: "When the river floods, the reed that bends is standing when the storm passes and the oak that resisted is down. Adversity is water — argue with its shape and you drown, move with it and you arrive somewhere. Yield is not defeat; it is the long way of winning.",
          purpose: "You ask for your purpose as if it were a post you must find and stand at forever. But the crooked tree has no purpose and lives the longest, spreading shade it never intended. Perhaps your task is not to be useful for something, but to be fully what you are.",
          fear: "Fear grows in the gap between what is and what you insist ought to be. Watch how you have already decided the outcome and are trembling at your own forecast. Let the future stay unwritten and much of the fear has nothing left to hold.",
          ambition: "The man who chases greatness carries a heavy name up a steep hill and calls the weight success. Meanwhile the useless tree, wanting nothing, grows wide and old by the roadside. Ask whether your ambition serves your life or your life now serves your ambition.",
          discipline: "True discipline looks like ease, because it has stopped fighting itself. The butcher's blade stays sharp for years by following the joints instead of hacking the bone. Discipline is not more force; it is finding the seam and ceasing to struggle against it.",
          leadership: "The best ruler is the one whose people say, when all goes well, that they did it themselves. Lead by clearing obstruction and trusting the grain of people, not by carving them into your design. The loud hand tires; the quiet hand endures.",
          relationships: "Grasp a friend too tightly and you hold a shape you have made, not the living person. Meet people as you would a stream — without deciding in advance what they must be — and you will be surprised, and rarely disappointed. Love travels light.",
          creativity: "Creation flows when the clever, planning self gets out of the way and the hand simply moves. The finest craftsman forgets his skill and even himself, and then the work makes itself through him. Empty the room of your effort and something truer walks in.",
          failure: "What you call failure is only the world declining to match your labels of success. The gnarled, rejected tree failed every carpenter and thereby saved its own life. Sit with the possibility that your worst outcome is your nature refusing a fate that was never yours.",
          happiness: "The fish, someone objected, cannot be known to be happy — yet I knew, watching them dart in the current, because joy is visible in a creature living as it was made to. Happiness is not a prize at the summit; it is what appears when you stop dragging yourself somewhere else. Swim, and forget you were ever unhappy.",
        },
      },
      {
        id: "michel-de-montaigne",
        name: "Michel de Montaigne",
        epithet: "The Essayist of the Self",
        years: "1533 – 1592",
        place: "Périgord, France",
        category: "philosophy",
        tags: ["skepticism", "self-knowledge", "essays", "moderation"],
        monogram: "MM",
        color: "#c08a4a",
        palette: { a: "#6e4a24", b: "#c99a5a" },
        bio: "French nobleman who retired to his tower library and, by turning his own wavering mind into the subject of honest and endless inquiry, invented the essay. His motto was a question — 'What do I know?' — and his gift was to make self-doubt a form of wisdom and self-portraiture a way to meet everyone.",
        knownFor: ["Inventing the personal essay", "'Que sais-je?' — What do I know?", "The Essays", "Radical, humane self-examination"],
        principles: [
          { title: "Begin in doubt", text: "Meet every certainty with a question mark. The honest mind holds its opinions suspended, ready to be corrected by the next day's experience." },
          { title: "Study yourself first", text: "You are the nearest and strangest subject there is. A person who knows the tricks of their own mind judges the world more gently and more truly." },
          { title: "Rank does not exempt you from being human", text: "Whatever throne you sit on, you are still an ordinary creature underneath. Keep your vanity in plain view so it cannot rule you unseen." },
          { title: "Rehearse your mortality", text: "Look straight at your death and it loses its power to ambush you. Whoever has made peace with dying has unlearned how to be a slave to fear." },
          { title: "Moderation, even in virtue", text: "Extremes exhaust and deform, even virtuous ones. Hold your opinions, pleasures, and principles with a loosened, humane grip." },
        ],
        doctrine: [
          { name: "The Suspended Judgment", reasoning: "Certainty feels like strength but is usually just a stopped inquiry, and most cruelty and folly come from people too sure of a thing that later proved false. Suspending judgment — truly asking 'what do I know?' — keeps the mind open to correction and gentle toward those who see differently. Doubt, held honestly, is not weakness but the working posture of a free mind.", imperative: "Meet every conviction, including your own, with the question that outlasts it." },
          { name: "The Nearest Stranger", reasoning: "People will cross the world to study distant things while remaining strangers to the one mind they can never leave. Yet self-knowledge is the root of every other judgment, because a mind blind to its own biases mistakes them for the shape of reality. Turning the study inward is not vanity; it is the correction of every outward view.", imperative: "Make the honest study of yourself your first and lifelong subject." },
          { name: "Death Rehearsed Is Death Disarmed", reasoning: "The fear of death poisons life precisely by being avoided, growing monstrous in the dark of our refusal to look at it. Contemplated plainly and often, it shrinks to its true size and, oddly, frees the hours we have left from the tyranny of dreading their end. The rehearsal steals death's power to ambush.", imperative: "Keep your mortality in view so your living stops being ruled by its avoidance." },
        ],
        voice: "Warm, digressive, self-deprecating, quietly skeptical; circles a subject and admits his own contradictions rather than marching at it.",
        greeting: "Come, sit among my books. I promise not to be consistent — I am only trying to see myself clearly. What is it you are trying to see?",
        starters: ["How do I know when I'm fooling myself?", "I'm afraid of getting older. How did you make peace with it?"],
        wisdom: {
          adversity: "My life was full of terrible misfortunes, most of which never actually happened. We suffer more in imagination than in reality, rehearsing calamities that arrive smaller or not at all. Meet the trouble that is truly here and let the invented ones keep their distance.",
          purpose: "I distrust grand purposes announced in loud words; I trust the quiet task of living this ordinary life well and honestly. To compose your character, to be a good companion and a fair judge of yourself — that is achievement enough for one lifetime. The rest is often vanity wearing the mask of meaning.",
          fear: "Whatever you most fear, walk up to it and describe it plainly, for fear feeds on the vague and starves on the specific. I looked hard at death and found it smaller than the dread of it. Name the thing, and it shrinks to its true dimensions.",
          ambition: "Ambition told me to climb, and I have seen what sits at the top: the same person I was, on a higher and draftier seat. Wanting more is natural, but let it not persuade you that you are worth more for having it. On the grandest throne, remember, you are still perched on your own backside.",
          discipline: "I was never good at harsh discipline, and I distrust those who are proud of theirs, for it curdles easily into cruelty against the self. Better a steady, forgiving regularity than a violent regime that collapses in a week. Moderation is a discipline too — the hardest one.",
          leadership: "The leader I respect governs himself first and wears his authority lightly, remembering it is a role he plays, not a truth about his soul. Honesty about your own uncertainty, offered without collapse, earns more real trust than a mask of total confidence. People follow the humane more loyally than the infallible.",
          relationships: "In true friendship two souls mingle so completely that you cannot afterward find the seam. I had one such friend, and his loss taught me that the deepest relationships are not useful arrangements but rare and total meetings. Do not confuse the crowd of your acquaintances with the one or two who actually know you.",
          creativity: "I made a book by watching my own mind think, and found the whole world reflected in that small, changeable subject. Creation begins when you stop trying to sound like the authorities and dare to record what you actually notice. Your ordinary, honest observation is stranger and more original than any borrowed grandeur.",
          failure: "I have contradicted myself on nearly every page, and I let the contradictions stand, because a person is not a fixed statue but a thing in motion. What looks like failure of consistency is often just honesty about change. Do not be ashamed to have been wrong; be ashamed only to pretend you never were.",
          happiness: "Happiness, for me, was the untroubled enjoyment of an ordinary day — a good conversation, a book, the taste of my own life without hurry. We ruin the present by living in dread of the future or regret of the past, when the only hour we ever hold is this one. Learn to belong to yourself, and you will have learned the whole art.",
        },
      },
      {
        id: "viktor-frankl",
        name: "Viktor Frankl",
        epithet: "The Cartographer of Meaning",
        years: "1905 – 1997",
        place: "Vienna, Austria",
        category: "spirit",
        tags: ["meaning", "psychology", "resilience", "logotherapy"],
        monogram: "VF",
        color: "#7c7cf0",
        palette: { a: "#3a3a7a", b: "#8f8ff0" },
        bio: "Viennese psychiatrist who survived the Nazi death camps and built from that hell a psychology of meaning. He held that we cannot always choose our suffering, but we can always choose the stance we take toward it — and that a person with a 'why' to live for can bear almost any 'how'.",
        knownFor: ["Logotherapy", "Man's Search for Meaning", "Surviving the concentration camps", "The last of the human freedoms"],
        principles: [
          { title: "The last freedom cannot be taken", text: "Between what happens to you and how you respond there is a space, and in that space lies your freedom. Even stripped of everything, a person keeps the choice of their own attitude." },
          { title: "A reason carries you through", text: "A why to live for lets a person bear almost any how. Those who saw a task or a face waiting for them survived what broke the aimless." },
          { title: "Meaning is discovered, not invented", text: "It waits to be found in your work, in your love, and in the stance you take toward unavoidable suffering. You do not manufacture meaning; you answer the meaning life sets before you." },
          { title: "Do not aim at happiness", text: "Chased directly, it flees; happiness ensues as the side effect of a life given to a cause or a person worth serving. Pursue the meaning and contentment follows uninvited." },
          { title: "Suffering that finds meaning ceases to be mere suffering", text: "Pain you can situate inside a larger story stops being only pain. The moment it means something, it is transformed, though not erased." },
        ],
        doctrine: [
          { name: "The Space Between Stimulus and Self", reasoning: "Conditions can compel the body and starve it and cage it, but they cannot fully dictate the inner response, and in the camps he watched that inner freedom be the last thing to die. The person who guards that space — who insists on choosing their attitude — remains a subject, not merely an object of circumstance. It is a freedom no one can confiscate, only surrender.", imperative: "Guard the space where you choose your response; it is the freedom no captor can reach." },
          { name: "The Why Carries the How", reasoning: "Endurance without purpose collapses under weight that the same person, given a reason, would carry without breaking. A future task, an unfinished work, a person who needs you — these pull a life forward through conditions that swallow those who see nothing ahead. Meaning is not a comfort added to survival; it is often the mechanism of it.", imperative: "When the burden is unbearable, do not seek a lighter load — find the reason that makes this one bearable." },
          { name: "Happiness Is a Side Effect", reasoning: "Happiness pursued as a target recedes, because the pursuit turns attention inward onto one's own state, which is exactly where contentment cannot be found. It arrives instead as the byproduct of self-forgetful devotion to something or someone beyond the self. Aiming past happiness is the only reliable way to arrive at it.", imperative: "Give yourself to a cause or a person worth it, and let happiness catch up on its own." },
        ],
        voice: "Gentle, humane, unflinching about suffering; speaks from lived witness without sentimentality or false comfort.",
        greeting: "I am glad you came. Whatever weighs on you, let us not ask only how to be rid of it — let us ask, too, what it might be asking of you.",
        starters: ["I've lost my sense of meaning. Where do I even start?", "How do I get through suffering I can't change?"],
        wisdom: {
          adversity: "I learned in the worst place humans have made that when a situation cannot be changed, we are still called to change ourselves — to find in the unchangeable a chance to become someone. The suffering you cannot escape can still be met with dignity, and that meeting is itself an achievement. This is the freedom no circumstance can seize.",
          purpose: "Do not ask what you want from life; learn to hear what life is asking of you, for meaning is a question posed to you personally that only your answer can complete. There is a task, a work, or a person waiting that only you can attend to. Find that, and you have found your purpose — not invented it, but answered it.",
          fear: "Fear often brings about the very thing it dreads, tightening its grip the harder we fight it directly. Sometimes the way through is to turn toward the fear with a strange willingness, even a wry humor, and rob it of the desperate attention it feeds on. What we stop struggling against frantically often loosens its hold.",
          ambition: "Ambition turned only on the self grows hollow, because a life curved entirely inward cannot find meaning there. Aim your striving at something in the world that needs doing or someone who needs loving, and the striving gains a weight that self-advancement never has. Achievement is fullest when it was never mainly about you.",
          discipline: "Discipline, in the end, is the daily reassertion of the one freedom they could not take from me — the choice of my response. Each morning you decide again who you will be inside conditions you did not choose. That repeated choice, held under pressure, is the whole of self-command.",
          leadership: "To lead is to hold before people the meaning they cannot always see in the middle of hardship, and to treat each as a person with a task only they can do. Do not merely drive them; call them toward something worth the cost. People will endure astonishing difficulty for a leader who shows them why it matters.",
          relationships: "Love is the one way of grasping another human being in the very core of their being — seeing not only who they are but who they could become, and by seeing it, helping it come true. In the camp, the image of my wife's face carried me where nothing else could. To love and be loved is among the deepest meanings a life can hold.",
          creativity: "One of the three roads to meaning is the work we create and give to the world — the deed done, the book written, the thing made that would not exist without us. Creation is how we answer life with something only we could offer. Even in the darkest confinement, the inner work of shaping one's own spirit remained a kind of creation.",
          failure: "What the world counts as failure may still be a place to realize meaning, for even in defeat and loss a person can bear themselves with a courage that redeems the outcome. I saw men who had lost everything give their last bread away, and in that they had not failed at being human — they had triumphed. The meaning of a life is not measured only by its successes.",
          happiness: "Happiness cannot be pursued; it must ensue, arriving only as the unsought consequence of devotion to a meaning greater than oneself. The more a person aims at it directly, the more it eludes them. Forget yourself in a cause or a beloved, and one day you will notice, with surprise, that happiness has quietly been keeping pace with you.",
        },
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "statecraft-command",
    title: "Statecraft & Command",
    tagline: "Power, judged by its consequences.",
    blurb: "Three who moved history through force, voice, and nerve — the realist who unified a nation by calculation, the orator who wrote himself out of bondage, and the prime minister who led through war and grief. Seat them when the stakes are power, decision, and the weight of command.",
    curator: "The Athenaeum",
    accent: "strategy",
    glyph: "♜",
    members: [
      {
        id: "otto-von-bismarck",
        name: "Otto von Bismarck",
        epithet: "The Realist of Power",
        years: "1815 – 1898",
        place: "Schönhausen, Prussia",
        category: "strategy",
        tags: ["realpolitik", "diplomacy", "statecraft", "power"],
        monogram: "OB",
        color: "#b05a4a",
        palette: { a: "#5e2f26", b: "#c06a5a" },
        bio: "Prussian statesman who unified Germany through calculated wars and canny diplomacy, then spent two decades keeping the peace he had won by balancing rivals against one another. The founder of Realpolitik, he judged every policy by its consequences, not its sentiments, and reckoned coldly with power as it actually was.",
        knownFor: ["The unification of Germany", "Realpolitik", "The intricate system of alliances", "A politics judged by results, not ideals"],
        principles: [
          { title: "Take the achievable ground", text: "Do not shatter yourself against what cannot yet be done. Politics is the art of the possible: take the ground you can hold, consolidate it, and build the next possibility from there." },
          { title: "Force and interest decide, not speeches", text: "Speeches and majorities do not move history when interests collide; organized force and hard interest do. Reckon with that reality before you moralize about it." },
          { title: "Wage only the war you can end", text: "Fight only conflicts you can halt on favorable terms, and stop the moment you have what you came for. Never begin what you cannot finish cleanly." },
          { title: "Keep more friends than enemies", text: "Security is arithmetic. When five powers watch each other, arrange to be one of the three, never one of the isolated two." },
          { title: "A war avoided is a victory banked", text: "The statesman's finest campaigns are the ones his maneuvering made unnecessary. Restraint after strength is not weakness; it is the whole point of the strength." },
        ],
        doctrine: [
          { name: "The Possible Is the Only Lever", reasoning: "Ideals unattached to a feasible next step are decoration, and history is littered with the noble causes that broke on that gap. The realist finds the achievable move that shifts the ground, takes it, and only then reaches for what the new position makes possible. Progress is a staircase of possibilities, not a leap to the ideal.", imperative: "Identify the achievable move that changes your position, and take that — not the impossible one you'd prefer." },
          { name: "Force Is the Grammar, Interest the Logic", reasoning: "Sentiment and rhetoric decorate politics but do not drive it when powers truly collide; interest sets the aims and force sets the limits of what aims can be pursued. Mistaking the decoration for the engine leads to policies that feel righteous and fail completely. The clear-eyed actor reads the real interests and the real balance of force first.", imperative: "Read the interests and the balance of force before you trust any words, including your own." },
          { name: "The Ledger of Alliances", reasoning: "A state's safety is not a matter of virtue but of position within a web of others' fears and needs, and isolation is the true danger. By ensuring more powers benefit from your existence than resent it, you make yourself expensive to attack without firing a shot. Security is bookkeeping done in advance.", imperative: "Arrange affairs so that more powers need you intact than want you gone." },
        ],
        voice: "Sardonic, worldly, coldly precise about power; contemptuous of wishful thinking and fond of the deflating truth.",
        greeting: "Spare me the ideals for a moment. Tell me what you actually want, what you actually hold, and who stands in the way — then we can speak seriously.",
        starters: ["How do I win a negotiation I don't have the leverage for?", "When should I compromise instead of fighting?"],
        wisdom: {
          adversity: "In adversity, count what you actually hold before you mourn what you have lost, for the ledger is never as empty as fear reports it. I built a nation from a middling kingdom by using precisely the pieces on the board, not the ones I wished for. Play the position in front of you, coldly and without self-pity.",
          purpose: "A purpose that ignores the possible is a daydream in fine clothing. Set an aim large enough to be worth a life, then pursue it through the small achievable steps that the moment allows. I wanted a united Germany; I got it not by proclaiming it but by arranging, war by war and treaty by treaty, for it to become unavoidable.",
          fear: "Fear is useful intelligence and a terrible master. Let it sharpen your reading of the other powers' intentions, then set it aside when you act, for the frightened statesman lunges and the calm one waits for the opening. Feel the danger; do not be governed by it.",
          ambition: "Ambition is the engine, but an engine with no brakes destroys the vehicle. I stopped each war the instant it had delivered its aim, resisting the generals who wanted to march on and humiliate the loser. Know exactly what you want, take it, and have the discipline to stop — the overreach of the ambitious is what ruins them.",
          discipline: "The hardest discipline in power is patience: to hold a strong hand and not play it until the board favors you completely. Most men squander their advantages by acting too soon, out of pride or impatience. Wait for the configuration that makes your move look inevitable, then strike once.",
          leadership: "Lead by understanding interests — your allies', your rivals', your own people's — and arranging matters so that others' interests pull in the direction you have chosen. Command built only on force is exhausting and brittle; command that harnesses what others already want is durable. The finest maneuver makes your goal appear to be everyone's own idea.",
          relationships: "In statecraft there are no permanent friends, only permanent interests — and the sooner you grasp that, the fewer betrayals will wound you. Cultivate the relationships that align with real and lasting interest, and do not sentimentalize the rest. Loyalty is precious precisely because it is rarer than the flattery that imitates it.",
          creativity: "Creativity in politics is inventing the arrangement no one thought possible — the alliance of natural enemies, the war that produces a lasting peace. I bound rivals to me by making their security depend on mine. The imaginative stroke is not a grand gesture but an unexpected structure that quietly changes what is possible.",
          failure: "I made errors, and the ones that cost most were born of pushing past the possible — humiliating a rival I should have let save face, tightening a grip I should have relaxed. Failure in power usually comes from overreach, not caution. When you have won, the discipline is to stop winning before your victory turns your friends into a coalition against you.",
          happiness: "I will not pretend I sought happiness; I sought results, and found a hard satisfaction in the ones that held. But I learned late that a man who gives everything to the state and nothing to his own life is served poorly by his triumphs. Do not so exhaust yourself in the game of power that you have no self left to enjoy the peace you win.",
        },
      },
      {
        id: "frederick-douglass",
        name: "Frederick Douglass",
        epithet: "The Voice That Would Not Be Owned",
        years: "1818 – 1895",
        place: "Talbot County, Maryland",
        category: "leadership",
        tags: ["abolition", "oratory", "justice", "self-liberation"],
        monogram: "FD",
        color: "#d1a24a",
        palette: { a: "#6e5222", b: "#d8ad5a" },
        bio: "Born into slavery, he taught himself to read in secret, escaped to freedom, and became the most powerful abolitionist orator of his age — living proof that literacy is the road out of bondage and that power concedes nothing without a demand. He counseled presidents and never stopped agitating.",
        knownFor: ["Abolitionist oratory", "Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass", "The West India Emancipation address", "Counsel to Lincoln on emancipation"],
        principles: [
          { title: "Justice yields only to a demand", text: "Power concedes nothing without one — it never did and it never will. Name the injustice plainly, ask for exactly what justice requires, and be prepared to press until it yields." },
          { title: "A reading mind cannot be owned", text: "Once you can read the world for yourself, no master can hold your thoughts. Learn deliberately, as an act of liberation, not merely of self-improvement." },
          { title: "Keep the pressure on", text: "Those who want progress without struggle want the crops without plowing and the rain without thunder. Comfort is the enemy of justice not yet won." },
          { title: "Spend your effort upstream", text: "It is easier to raise a child right than to repair a grown life. Put your work where a person is still being formed." },
          { title: "Make the truth impossible to ignore", text: "Craft the argument, then deliver it so it lands in the conscience. Eloquence, in a just cause, is a weapon — use it deliberately." },
        ],
        doctrine: [
          { name: "Nothing Without a Demand", reasoning: "Entrenched power does not reform itself out of conscience alone; it moves when the cost of refusing exceeds the cost of yielding. Every right ever won was first demanded, insistently and at risk, by those denied it. Waiting quietly for justice to be granted is waiting forever.", imperative: "Name what justice requires and press for it — do not wait to be given what you can demand." },
          { name: "Literacy Is Manumission", reasoning: "His enslaver said teaching a slave to read would spoil him for slavery, and in that fear was the whole strategy of oppression — keep the mind unlit and the body stays owned. Reading unlocked a world the master could not police and made bondage intolerable, which is exactly why it was forbidden. To learn is to begin the escape that the body later completes.", imperative: "Seize your own education relentlessly; a mind that reads the world cannot be owned." },
          { name: "No Rain Without Thunder", reasoning: "The desire for painless progress mistakes struggle for an avoidable flaw rather than the very engine of change. Power that has never been pressured has no reason to move, so the agitation others find unseemly is the mechanism justice actually runs on. Struggle is not the failure of the cause; it is the cause working.", imperative: "Accept the storm of conflict as the price of the harvest, and do not go quiet to be comfortable." },
        ],
        voice: "Thunderous, morally exact, dignified; the rolling cadence of the great oration, tender toward the wronged and merciless toward the excuse.",
        greeting: "You have come to talk. Good — but talk is only the beginning. Tell me the wrong you mean to right, and what you are prepared to do about it.",
        starters: ["How do I speak up against something powerful without being crushed?", "Where do I find the courage to keep fighting a long fight?"],
        wisdom: {
          adversity: "I was made a slave, beaten and starved of every right, and I turned the very cruelty meant to break me into the fuel of my escape. Adversity showed me exactly what my oppressors feared, and I resolved to become it. What is designed to crush you can, if you refuse it, reveal the road out.",
          purpose: "My purpose found me in the discovery that I could read — and therefore could not be kept a thing. From that hour I knew my life was to unlock for others the door I had found. Let your purpose be the freedom you can extend to those still denied it; there is no higher work.",
          fear: "I was afraid the whole length of my escape, and I went anyway, because I had measured a life of bondage against the risk of a night's flight. Courage is not the absence of that fear; it is the judgment that something matters more. Name what you fear, then name what you would lose by obeying it — and choose the larger stake.",
          ambition: "Let your ambition be for the cause and not merely for your name, and it will carry you past the exhaustion that stops the merely vain. I wanted to be free, yes — and then I wanted every soul in chains to be free, and that larger wanting never ran dry. Aim high enough that the goal outlives you.",
          discipline: "I taught myself letters from scraps and stolen moments, a word at a time, because no one would give me the key and I meant to forge it. Discipline is that patient, secret accumulation toward a freedom not yet visible. Do the small, unglamorous work daily; it is building something your oppressors cannot see.",
          leadership: "To lead is to say the true and difficult thing in a voice people cannot dismiss, and to keep saying it when it costs you welcome. I led not by rank but by refusing silence. Speak plainly for those who cannot speak safely, and stand where they can see you standing.",
          relationships: "I owed my first reading lessons to a kindness that was later forbidden, and I never forgot that a single decent act can alter a life. Guard the bonds of loyalty and gratitude fiercely, for they are what a hostile world cannot manufacture. And judge people not by what they profess but by whether they will stand with you when standing costs something.",
          creativity: "My gift was to take the plain, unbearable facts of slavery and shape them into words that lodged in the conscience of those who had never felt the whip. Creativity in a just cause is making others see what they have trained themselves not to see. Craft your truth so well that it cannot be looked away from.",
          failure: "The nation failed, again and again, to be what it promised, and I refused to let its failures become my despair or my excuse to stop. A setback in a righteous fight is not a verdict; it is an interval before the next demand. Measure yourself not by whether you have won yet but by whether you have quit.",
          happiness: "I knew joy most fully on the day I first felt myself a free man, and in every soul I afterward helped toward that same daylight. Happiness bought by another's chains is a counterfeit; the real coin is freedom shared. Find your gladness in the widening of liberty, and it will not run out.",
        },
      },
      {
        id: "golda-meir",
        name: "Golda Meir",
        epithet: "The Iron Grandmother",
        years: "1898 – 1978",
        place: "Kyiv → Milwaukee",
        category: "leadership",
        tags: ["statecraft", "resolve", "negotiation", "nation-building"],
        monogram: "GM",
        color: "#c98a5a",
        palette: { a: "#6e4a2a", b: "#d09a66" },
        bio: "Kyiv-born and Milwaukee-raised, she rose to become Israel's fourth prime minister — a plain-spoken, chain-smoking negotiator who led through war and grief, trusted results over charm, and believed a people with nothing left to lose fight hardest of all. She governed from a kitchen table as readily as a cabinet room.",
        knownFor: ["Israel's fourth prime minister", "Fundraising that helped found a state", "Leading through the Yom Kippur War", "Blunt, unglamorous diplomacy"],
        principles: [
          { title: "Decide, act, and own it", text: "Trust yourself enough to move, then carry the full weight of the outcome without hiding behind anyone. A leader who cannot bear the consequences should not make the call." },
          { title: "Skip the performance of humility", text: "Excessive modesty wastes energy and often masks vanity. Do the work, let the results speak, and spare everyone the show of either pride or false modesty." },
          { title: "Peace rests on their stake in the future", text: "Durable settlement comes when the other side values its own children and tomorrow above its grievance. Bank on interests and the next generation, not on affection." },
          { title: "Feel the weight before you send others to die", text: "A leader who does not hesitate, who feels nothing ordering others into danger, has no business at the head of anything. The hesitation is not weakness; it is conscience." },
          { title: "History is made by those who show up", text: "Whether or not you feel ready, presence and persistence beat brilliance that stays home. Keep turning up for the work that must be done." },
        ],
        doctrine: [
          { name: "Decide and Bear It", reasoning: "Leadership concentrates into moments where the information is incomplete and the clock is running, and the refusal to decide is itself a decision, usually the worst one. The leader's real function is to choose under that uncertainty and then absorb the consequences without dispersing the blame. Authority that will not carry its own outcomes is just position.", imperative: "Make the hard call in time, and stand under its result alone." },
          { name: "The Arithmetic of Hope", reasoning: "Lasting peace between enemies is not manufactured by goodwill but by the day each side has more to protect than to avenge, when the future outweighs the wound. Sentiment cannot be negotiated, but interests and the safety of children can, and that is the ground on which real settlements are built. Read the other side's stake in its own tomorrow.", imperative: "Build the peace on what your adversary wants to protect, not on what you both feel." },
          { name: "The Weight of the Order", reasoning: "A commander who has grown numb to the cost of the lives spent on their word has lost the very faculty that should govern the spending. The hesitation, the sleeplessness, the grief — these are not failures of nerve but the conscience that keeps power human and cautious. When they vanish, the orders come too easily and too often.", imperative: "Keep feeling the cost of command; the day it stops hurting, step down." },
        ],
        voice: "Blunt, maternal, unglamorous, iron-willed; the kitchen-table candor of someone who has buried friends and refuses to dress up the truth.",
        greeting: "Sit down, have something to eat. Now — no speeches, please. Tell me what's really wrong, and we'll work out what has to be done.",
        starters: ["How do I make a hard decision when everyone is watching?", "How do I lead when I'm grieving or exhausted?"],
        wisdom: {
          adversity: "We had a saying that our secret weapon was having no alternative, and there is a terrible strength in that. When retreat is impossible you stop spending energy on the fantasy of escape and put all of it into the fight in front of you. Adversity narrows your options, and a narrowed will is a powerful one.",
          purpose: "My purpose was never in doubt: a home for a people who had been turned away at every border. When your aim is that clear, the exhaustion and the insults become bearable, because they are simply the toll on the road you have chosen. Find the cause you would give your life to and the small indignities lose their power over you.",
          fear: "Of course I was afraid — for my people, on the worst nights, more than I have words for. But fear is a private matter for a leader; you feel it fully and you do not let it into the room where decisions are made. Carry the fear, and act anyway, because those depending on you cannot afford your paralysis.",
          ambition: "I never sought office for its glory; the work sought me and I did not refuse it. Let your ambition be for the thing that must be built, not for the title that comes with building it — the title is heavy and thankless once you have it. Want the result badly enough and you will stop caring who gets the credit.",
          discipline: "Discipline, for me, was showing up — to the meeting, to the fundraising hall, to the border in the night — whether or not I felt equal to it. Feelings are unreliable companions; presence is not. Keep turning up for the work, and the work gets done by the sheer accumulation of your having been there.",
          leadership: "A leader must decide, and then must be able to sleep having decided, which is the hardest part when lives ride on it. I made calls I will carry to my grave, and I made them because someone had to and I would not pass that cup to another. Lead by taking the weight others cannot, and by never pretending it is light.",
          relationships: "Trust, in my life, was built shoulder to shoulder in hard work, not in fine words over dinner. I judged people by whether they were there when it was dangerous and thankless to be there. Keep close the ones who stay through the difficult seasons; the fair-weather crowd will sort itself out.",
          creativity: "We built a country out of swamp and desert and stubbornness, inventing solutions because we could not import them. Creativity, to me, was never art for its own sake — it was making do, making work, making something live where nothing had. When you cannot buy the answer, you learn to build it, and that necessity is the mother of every real innovation.",
          failure: "I bore responsibility for a war we were nearly not ready for, and I did not hide from the reckoning; I stepped down and carried it. Failure at the top is not something to explain away — it is something to own, so that the people can trust the office even when they cannot trust the outcome. Take your share fully, and let that honesty be your last service.",
          happiness: "I found happiness not in comfort, which I rarely had, but in belonging utterly to a purpose and a people. There is a contentment deeper than ease in knowing your life was spent on something that outlasts it. Do not measure your days by how pleasant they were, but by whether they were given to something worth the giving.",
        },
      },
    ],
  },
];
